export const WEEK_INDEX = {"HINDUISM":{"1":{"day":1,"title":"the one at every doorway","word":"Ganesha","hook":"an elephant-headed figure on the dashboard, by the door, at the top of the page","length":"3:00","carry":"clear the road, then begin","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY ONE.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Every Hindu beginning starts with him, so this one does too. A small statue on a taxi dashboard. A picture above a shop counter. His name printed at the top of a wedding invitation, before anyone else's. Elephant head, round belly, one tusk broken. He sits wherever things start, and that's the clue to who he is.","screen":["`EVERY BEGINNING STARTS WITH HIM.` → `DASHBOARDS. DOORWAYS. INVITATIONS.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min","voice":"Start with the name. Ganesha is gana plus isha: lord of the ganas, the attendants of the god Shiva. But the title that explains the doorways is Vighnaharta. Vighna, an obstacle; harta, one who removes. The remover of obstacles. That's why he's honored first: before a ceremony, a wedding, a new shop or an exam. Some people write shri ganeshaya namah at the top of a page before they begin: namah means a bow. There's a popular way of reading his body: big ears, listen more than you talk; small eyes, focus; a big belly, take in what life serves, good and hard. And the broken tusk: tradition says he wrote down the Mahabharata as the sage Vyasa dictated it, and one story says that when his pen gave out, he snapped off his own tusk and kept writing.","screen":["`LORD OF THE GANAS.` · `VIGHNAHARTA — REMOVER OF OBSTACLES.` · `SHRI GANESHAYA NAMAH.` · `THE BROKEN TUSK.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"15 sec","voice":"One breath with the bell. Rest a hand on something that marks a start: a door, a laptop, a notebook. Name, once, what's in the way of the next thing you're beginning. Then breathe out and lift your hand.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Ganesha. Lord of the ganas, remover of obstacles, the one at the door. First word on your strand, and the first name.","screen":["`GANESHA — your 1st word` → clicks onto an empty word-strand."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: clear the road, then begin. It's why his name comes first on so many invitations, and why he came first here. See you tomorrow.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 1 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE SOUND THE UNIVERSE HUMS.`"]}]},"2":{"day":2,"title":"the sound the universe hums","word":"om","hook":"the sound a room makes together; it's three sounds and a silence","length":"3:00","carry":"one sound, before words","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY TWO.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: Ganesha, the one who goes first. Today, the sound that opens almost every Hindu prayer.","screen":["`GANESHA ✓` → `DAY 2.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Om. A yoga class ends with it, and a lot of Hindu prayers begin with it. Most people treat it as one long hum. One of the oldest Hindu texts hears four parts in it: three sounds, and a fourth that isn't a sound at all. First, a guess.","screen":["`OM.` → `ONE HUM?` → `FOUR PARTS.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min","voice":"Say it slowly and you can feel three sounds. A, with the mouth open. U, as the lips round. M, as they close. That's why it's sometimes written aum. An old text of just twelve verses starts by saying om is all of this: past, present, future, and whatever lies beyond time. Then it maps the sounds onto your day. A is waking, the world you can see and touch. U is dreaming, the world made of the mind. M is deep, dreamless sleep, where nothing is wanted. The fourth part has no sound. It's the silence after the m runs out, and the text calls it simply the fourth: the self, at peace. So om isn't a sign-off. It's a map of a whole day in one breath, with the quiet underneath it. The Bhagavad Gita says acts of offering, giving and discipline begin with it.","screen":["`A — WAKING.` · `U — DREAMING.` · `M — DEEP SLEEP.` · `THE FOURTH — THE SILENCE.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"20 sec","voice":"One breath with the bell. Breathe in. Let it out as the sound, aloud or under your breath: a, u, m. Let the m run out on its own, then stay a second in the quiet after it. That quiet is the fourth part.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Om. A, u and m for waking, dreaming and deep sleep, and the silence after it for the fourth. Second word on your strand.","screen":["`OM — your 2nd word` → clicks onto the strand beside namaste."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: one sound, before words. Not a task. Something to notice the next time a room goes quiet before it begins. Day two, done.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 2 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE PRAYER YOU KNOW BY HEART.`"]}]},"3":{"day":3,"title":"the prayer you know by heart","word":"Gayatri","hook":"the prayer you can say start to finish, and what it asks for","length":"3:00","carry":"a prayer for a clear mind","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY THREE.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: om, three sounds and a silence. Today it opens a prayer you might already know by heart.","screen":["`OM.` → `DAY 3.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Om bhur bhuvah svah. If you grew up in a Hindu home, there's a good chance you can say the rest without thinking. Maybe someone said it every morning. Maybe it played at every puja. Now the hard part: what does it mean? Most people go quiet. Today, line by line. First, a guess.","screen":["`OM BHUR BHUVAH SVAH…` → `YOU CAN SAY IT.` → `WHAT DOES IT MEAN?`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min","voice":"It's called the Gayatri mantra, and it's one of the oldest verses Hindus still say every day. Take it a line at a time. Om: the sound from yesterday. Bhur bhuvah svah: earth, sky, and everything beyond: the whole world. Tat savitur varenyam: that bright, worthy light of the sun. Bhargo devasya dhimahi: we meditate on that divine radiance. Dhiyo yo nah prachodayat: may it wake up and guide our minds. Notice what it asks for. Not money. Not luck. Not to win. A clear mind. That's what a mantra is: the word comes from man, to think, and tra, a tool. A tool for the mind, said on purpose. Many families say the Gayatri at dawn, as the light comes up, and some say it a set number of times on beads. Once you know the lines, it stops being a sound you repeat and becomes a request you mean.","screen":["`OM — THE SOUND FROM YESTERDAY.` · `EARTH, SKY, AND BEYOND.` · `MAY IT WAKE UP OUR MINDS.` · `A TOOL FOR THE MIND.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Three breaths with the bell. Breathe in. As you breathe out, say the Gayatri once, slowly, out loud or in your head, or just its last line: dhiyo yo nah prachodayat, may it wake up and guide our minds. This time, mean the words.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Gayatri. The morning prayer for a clear mind, and a mantra: a tool for the mind. Third word on your strand.","screen":["`GAYATRI — your 3rd word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: a prayer for a clear mind. Next time you hear it, you'll know what's being asked. Day three.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 3 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE OLDEST HELLO.`"]}]},"4":{"day":4,"title":"the oldest hello","word":"namaste","hook":"you've said it after yoga class; the words inside say more than hello","length":"3:00","carry":"the light in me sees the light in you","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY FOUR.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: the Gayatri, a prayer for a clear mind. Today, a word you've said a thousand times as hello.","screen":["`A PRAYER FOR A CLEAR MIND.` → `DAY 4.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"You've said it at the end of a yoga class, and you've probably heard it means the light in me sees the light in you. The word itself says something much simpler. The longer meaning is a reading of it, and that reading comes from somewhere specific. First, a guess.","screen":["`NAMASTE.` → `WHAT IT SAYS.` → `WHAT IT'S READ AS.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 10","voice":"Namaste is Sanskrit, and it splits in two. Namas: a bow. Te: to you. Word for word, I bow to you. That's the whole dictionary meaning. So where does the light come from? From a reading of the word. One of the oldest Hindu texts describes a light that shines above the heavens, above everything, and then says that same light is the light inside a person. Out there and in here: one light. Put that next to a bow and you get the popular line: the light in me sees the light in you. Not a translation. An interpretation, with a real source behind it. What the reading changes is who you're bowing to. Not someone's job, mood or résumé, but whatever in them is the same as what's in you. And nobody has to earn it first. In this reading, it's already there. The same bow goes further at home. When a kid touches a grandparent's feet at the door, that's pranam, from pra, forward, and nam, to bend: the same root as namaste. And the elder's hand comes down on the child's head: a blessing, given back. Respect goes one way, a blessing comes the other.","screen":["`NAMAS — A BOW.` · `TE — TO YOU.` · `THE SAME LIGHT, INSIDE A PERSON.` · `A BLESSING, GIVEN BACK.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Three breaths with the bell. Palms together if you like, or just sit up straight. Breathe in. As you breathe out, tip your head slightly forward: the bow the word is named for. Nothing to feel, nothing to get right. Just the gesture, done once, on purpose.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Namaste. Word for word, I bow to you. Read deeper, the light in me sees the light in you. Fourth word on your strand.","screen":["`NAMASTE — your 4th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: the light in me sees the light in you. Not a task. Just a way to look at the next person you meet. That's day four.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 4 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: LIGHT PASSED HAND TO HAND.`"]}]},"5":{"day":5,"title":"light passed hand to hand","word":"arti","hook":"a flame on a plate, moved in circles, then carried around the room","length":"3:00","carry":"light passed hand to hand","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY FIVE.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: namaste, I bow to you, and the bow that touches an elder's feet. Today, the flame that comes around on a plate.","screen":["`I BOW TO YOU.` → `DAY 5.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"Evening at a temple. Or the steps down to a river, as dusk comes in. Or a living room, when the lamp comes out. A small flame sits on a metal plate, and someone moves it in slow circles in front of the shrine. A bell rings. People sing. Then the plate is carried around the room, and one by one, people pass their hands over the flame and bring their palms to their eyes and forehead. It takes a few minutes, start to finish. Today's word is that flame, and what happens when it leaves the shrine.","screen":["`A FLAME ON A PLATE.` → `SLOW CIRCLES. A BELL.` → `THEN IT COMES TO THE PEOPLE.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 5","voice":"Arti. The waving of a lamp; the offering of light. After the flowers and food are offered, the lamp is circled before the image of God, and a song is sung. The best known begins: Om jai jagadish hare. Victory to you, lord of the world. An old Hindu text has a line that helps with what the lamp might mean: the sun doesn't shine there, nor the moon and stars, let alone this little fire; everything shines after that one shining. So a small flame held up before God is light offered back to where light comes from. Then the flame leaves the shrine and goes out to the people. Each person draws its warmth toward their face. The same small fire touches every face in the room, and nobody gets more of it. Often there's one more step: prasad. Food or sweets offered before God, then handed out. The sweet you got after temple as a kid was that: an offering, given back as a blessing.","screen":["`ARTI — THE OFFERING OF LIGHT` · `EVERYTHING SHINES AFTER THAT ONE` · `THE SAME FIRE TOUCHES EVERY FACE` · `PRASAD — GIVEN BACK AS A BLESSING`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Three breaths with the bell. If there's a candle or a lamp near you, look at it; any light in the room will do. Cup your hands toward it, as if gathering warmth, and bring them to your eyes and forehead. Then bring one person to mind and turn your cupped hands toward them, as if passing the plate.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Arti. The offering of light, and then the light shared with everyone. Fifth word.","screen":["`ARTI — your 5th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: light passed hand to hand. Next time the plate comes around, you'll know what you're doing. Day five.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 5 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: WHY SO MANY GODS?.`"]}]},"6":{"day":6,"title":"why so many gods?","word":"ishta devata","hook":"why there are so many gods, and which one is yours","length":"3:00","carry":"one truth, many names","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY SIX.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: arti, light passed hand to hand. Today, the question every Hindu kid gets asked at school.","screen":["`LIGHT PASSED HAND TO HAND.` → `DAY 6.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Someone at school or work asks it, half curious, half teasing: how many gods do you have? Ganesha on the dashboard, Krishna in the calendar, Durga at Navratri, Lakshmi at Diwali, a whole shelf at your grandparents' place. It's a fair question, and most people never got a good answer to give. Here's one. First, a guess.","screen":["`HOW MANY GODS DO YOU HAVE?` → `A FAIR QUESTION.` → `HERE'S AN ANSWER.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min","voice":"One of the oldest Hindu verses says it in five words: ekam sat vipra bahudha vadanti. Truth is one; the wise call it by many names. That line is how a lot of Hindus answer the question. The many gods are many faces and names of one reality, the way one person can be a mother, a boss and a friend and still be one person. Not every Hindu puts it the same way. Some families give their whole devotion to Vishnu, or Shiva, or the Goddess, and see the others as part of that one. Some honor many, each for a different part of life. Almost everyone has an ishta devata: a chosen deity, the form of God that feels like yours. Often it comes from your family. Sometimes you find your own. So the answer to the question at school can be simple: one truth, many names, and one of them feels like home.","screen":["`TRUTH IS ONE.` · `THE WISE CALL IT BY MANY NAMES.` · `MANY FACES, ONE REALITY.` · `THE ONE THAT FEELS LIKE YOURS.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Three breaths with the bell. Think of the image of God you saw most growing up: on a wall, a dashboard, a shelf. Breathe in. As you breathe out, say its name once, quietly, as if it were the name of someone who knows you.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Ishta devata. Your chosen deity: the form of God that feels like yours. Sixth word.","screen":["`ISHTA DEVATA — your 6th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: one truth, many names. Next time someone asks how many gods you have, you've got an answer. Day six.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 6 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: NOT FATE, NOT PAYBACK.`"]}]},"7":{"day":7,"title":"not fate, not payback","word":"karma","hook":"the word everyone borrows, and what it actually says","length":"3:00","carry":"what you do, you become","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY SEVEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: one truth, many names, and the one that's yours. Six words so far: Ganesha, om, Gayatri, namaste, arti, ishta devata. Today, the seventh.","screen":["`GANESHA · OM · GAYATRI · NAMASTE · ARTI · ISHTA DEVATA`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"Karma. Someone says it when the car that cut them off gets stopped at the next light. Someone says it when a person who was unkind finally has a bad week. It turns up in song lyrics, on coffee mugs, under photos. Used like that, it means payback: the universe keeping a ledger and settling it on schedule, ideally where you can watch. It's a satisfying idea. It is also not quite what the word means. The older meaning is less about getting even and more about getting made, and it turns the whole thing around to face the one who's using it.","screen":["`KARMA.` → `THE CAR AT THE RED LIGHT.` → `PAYBACK?` → `NOT QUITE.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min 10","voice":"Karma is the Sanskrit word for action. A deed. Something done. In the Bhagavad Gita, Krishna points out that no one can stay without acting, even for a moment: breathing, eating, speaking, choosing, all of it is action. So the first thing to know is that karma isn't something that happens to you. It's what you do. The second thing is what action does back. One of the oldest Hindu texts says it like a proverb: as a person acts, so that person becomes. Not: so the person is punished. Becomes. Patience, practiced, turns into a patient person. Sharpness, practiced, turns into a sharp one. A mango seed isn't judged worthy of a mango tree; it grows one because that's what the seed is. The payback version keeps your eyes on other people: did they get what's coming? The older version turns you around: what am I growing with my next word? Hindu teachers also speak of karma across lifetimes, and that's a bigger conversation for later. For today: as you act, so you become.","screen":["`KARMA — ACTION, A DEED` · `NO ONE STAYS WITHOUT ACTING` · `AS ONE ACTS, SO ONE BECOMES` · `WHAT AM I GROWING?`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Three breaths with the bell. Bring to mind one small thing you did this week: a kind word, a sharp one, a promise kept or skipped. Don't grade it. Just ask what it's growing. Then pick one small action you'd be glad to see grow.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Karma. Action, and what action makes of the one who does it. Not payback. Seventh word: a week's worth.","screen":["`KARMA — your 7th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: what you do, you become. That's week one: seven words, and what's inside each. Tomorrow, what your grandmother was actually doing.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 7 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: WHAT YOUR GRANDMOTHER WAS ACTUALLY DOING.`"]}]},"8":{"day":8,"title":"what your grandmother was actually doing","word":"puja","hook":"a flower, a flame, a bell: what's happening at the home shrine","length":"6:00","carry":"attention is an offering","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY EIGHT.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: karma. Your line was what you do, you become. Week one is behind you. Week two starts at home. Day eight.","screen":["`WHAT YOU DO, YOU BECOME.` → `WEEK TWO.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"Before the kettle goes on, someone lights a small brass lamp. Beside it: a marigold, fresh this morning, a bell with a handle worn smooth, a stick of incense, a little water in a cup, a sweet on a tiny plate. Some Hindu homes keep a shelf like this, or a corner, or a whole small room, and someone stops there each morning. Walk past it on an ordinary morning and it can look like decoration. Stand and watch for a minute, though, and the person in front of it is busy: pouring, lighting, ringing, setting things down. Their hands are working through something in order. Today's word is the name for what they're doing.","screen":["`A LAMP. A FLOWER. A BELL.` → `SOMEONE STOPS THERE EACH MORNING.` → `WHAT ARE THEY DOING?`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"Puja. It's usually translated worship, and that's not wrong, but in English the word can sound formal and far away. A home puja is close and small, and it's done with the hands. Watch the steps. Water is offered. A flower is set down. Incense is lit. A lamp is waved. A bell is rung. Food is offered, and afterward it comes back as prasad, a gift to be eaten. Families and regions do it differently, and some teachers describe the whole thing as welcoming God as an honored guest. Now listen to the Bhagavad Gita. Krishna, speaking as God, says that whoever offers him a leaf, a flower, a fruit, or water, with devotion, he accepts it. In Sanskrit it begins: patram pushpam phalam toyam. A leaf. A flower. A fruit. Water. Nothing on that list costs much. What the verse cares about is the devotion, the heart that comes with the gift. So here's one way in, for anyone, whatever they believe. Puja gives a person's attention one place to land, and walks it through one sense after another: the cool of the water, the color of the flower, the smell of the smoke, the sound of the bell, the taste of the sweet. For those few minutes, the whole person is in one place. Maybe that's why the shrine can be so simple: a picture, a small stone, a lamp. The size of the offering was never the point. The care is. Seen that way, a grandmother at that shelf every morning isn't only keeping a custom. She's spending her attention on purpose, before anything else can claim it.","screen":["`WATER, FLOWER, FLAME, BELL, FOOD` · `A LEAF, A FLOWER, A FRUIT, WATER` · `ONE SENSE AFTER ANOTHER` · then, large: `ATTENTION IS AN OFFERING.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min 30","voice":"Here's the practice: honor one ordinary thing. No shelf needed. Look around and choose one object you can see: a cup, a plant, your own open hand. Give it your eyes first, its color and its edges. Then, if you can reach it, your touch. Then close your eyes and keep the picture of it. If you pray, you can offer this small attention to God. If you don't, just let one thing have all of you for a little while. The bell holds the time.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Puja. Honor, offered with the hands and the senses: a leaf, a flower, a fruit, water. Eighth word.","screen":["`PUJA — your 8th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: attention is an offering. Today, give one ordinary thing your whole attention: a meal, a face, a cup of tea. One minute, nothing else in your hands. Day eight.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 8 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE THING THAT'S YOURS TO DO.`"]}]},"9":{"day":9,"title":"the thing that's yours to do","word":"dharma","hook":"a warrior puts down his bow and says he no longer knows what's right","length":"3:00","carry":"do the thing that's yours to do","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY NINE.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: puja. Your line was attention is an offering. Today, the word at the center of the Bhagavad Gita.","screen":["`ATTENTION IS AN OFFERING.` → `DAY 9.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"25 sec","voice":"The Bhagavad Gita opens on a battlefield. A warrior named Arjuna asks his charioteer to drive him out between the two armies, and he sees his own family on the other side: teachers, uncles, cousins. His bow slips from his hand and he sits down in the chariot. The word he reaches for, to say what he's lost, is today's word.","screen":["`KURUKSHETRA.` → `THE BOW SLIPS.` → `WHAT HE'S LOST.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"1 min","voice":"Dharma gets translated as duty, law or religion, and each of those catches only a piece. It comes from a Sanskrit root meaning to hold, or to support. Dharma is what holds things up: teachers say the dharma of fire is to burn and the dharma of water is to flow. It's what a thing is for, the part it holds in the whole. In the Gita's second chapter, Arjuna says his mind is confused about dharma, and he asks his charioteer, Krishna, to teach him. The rest of the Gita is that teaching. One line from the third chapter shows where it starts: better your own dharma, even done imperfectly, than someone else's done well. Your own dharma is svadharma. So dharma isn't only rules handed down. It's also a question: what is mine to do here? Arjuna isn't missing information. He's lost the thread of what's his to do.","screen":["`DHARMA — WHAT HOLDS THINGS UP.` · `I AM YOUR STUDENT. TEACH ME.` · `YOUR OWN, EVEN IMPERFECTLY.` · `WHAT IS MINE TO DO HERE?`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Three breaths with the bell. On the first, bring to mind one thing you've been putting off. On the second, ask it once, silently: what's mine to do here? On the third, let the question go. No plan needed yet.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Dharma. From a root meaning to hold: what holds things up, and your share of the holding. Ninth word.","screen":["`DHARMA — your 9th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: do the thing that's yours to do. It's the question the whole Gita answers, and this path comes back to it. Day nine.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 9 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE ONE BEHIND YOUR EYES.`"]}]},"10":{"day":10,"title":"the one behind your eyes","word":"atman","hook":"what stays when everything else about you changes","length":"6:00","carry":"someone is in there","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY TEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: dharma. Your line was do the thing that's yours to do. Today we turn from what gets done to the one doing it. Day ten.","screen":["`DO THE THING THAT'S YOURS TO DO.` → `DAY 10.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"A question with no trick in it. Who is hearing this sentence? Not what. Who. The quick answer is: me. But try pointing at the me. The body? It has been changing since childhood. The mood? It may have shifted twice since breakfast. The thoughts? They're moving faster than this voice. Everything you can point to keeps moving. And yet, the Upanishads say, something has been there for all of it, the one all of it happened to. A teacher in an old Hindu text was pressed on exactly this, and his answer turned the question around. Today's word is what he was pointing at.","screen":["`WHO IS HEARING THIS?` → `NOT THE BODY. NOT THE MOOD.` → `SOMETHING HAS BEEN THERE FOR ALL OF IT.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"Atman. The self. Not the self in selfie, the personality you show the world. The Upanishads use atman for the deepest you: the one that is aware. And they notice something strange about it. You can't look straight at it. In the Brihadaranyaka Upanishad, a questioner keeps pressing the teacher Yajnavalkya: show me this self, plainly, the way you'd point and say, that's a cow, that's a horse. And Yajnavalkya answers, in effect: you can't see the seer of seeing. You can't hear the hearer of hearing. The one who looks can't become one more thing on the table to be looked at. So, in other passages, the same Upanishad describes the self by saying what it is not: neti, neti. Not this, not this. Whatever you can point to, the body, the breath, the busy mind, the self is not that. One way to put the Upanishad's point: take away everything that can be watched, and what's left is the watching. Put plainly: someone is in there. Today that someone gets its name. Here's where it turns practical. When worry comes, it comes as something seen: a thought, a tight chest, a story about tomorrow. The Upanishad points past it, to the one who sees. Hindu schools describe the atman in different ways. Some teach that the self is one with the whole. Others teach that it is a distinct soul, forever dear to God and dependent on God. Tomorrow brings the word for the whole. Today, just the turn this word makes: away from what's being watched, toward the one who is watching.","screen":["`ATMAN — THE SELF THAT IS AWARE` · `THE SEER OF SEEING` · `NETI, NETI — NOT THIS, NOT THIS` · `THE WATCHING, NOT THE WATCHED` · then, large: `SOMEONE IS IN THERE.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min 30","voice":"Today's practice is to notice the noticer. Let your eyes close or soften. Let a thought arrive, any thought. Don't follow it. Just see that it's there. Now notice that something saw it. Rest there for a moment, not in the thought, in the seeing. Another thought will come; that's what thoughts do. Same move: see it, then notice the one who saw. No need to get anywhere. The bell holds the time.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Atman. The self: not the body, not the thoughts, but the one aware of them. Tenth word.","screen":["`ATMAN — your 10th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: someone is in there. When worry shows up today, ask once, quietly: who is seeing this? Just the question. Day ten.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 10 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE OCEAN THE WAVE FORGETS IT IS.`"]}]},"11":{"day":11,"title":"the ocean the wave forgets it is","word":"Brahman","hook":"salt in a cup of water, and rivers that reach the sea","length":"6:15","carry":"not two","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY ELEVEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Yesterday: atman, the one behind your eyes. Your line was someone is in there. Did you catch yourself watching a thought? Today the word gets bigger, so we'll go slow. Day eleven.","screen":["`SOMEONE IS IN THERE.` → `DAY 11.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"A father hands his son a lump of salt and says: put this in a cup of water, and come back in the morning. The son does. Next morning the father says: bring me the salt. The boy looks. It's gone. Sip from the top, says the father. Salty. From the middle. Salty. From the bottom. Salty. The salt is in every sip, and he can't find it anywhere. The father is Uddalaka, the son is Shvetaketu, and the story is told in the Chandogya Upanishad. It's one of the old ways of pointing at today's word, and the word is a big one.","screen":["`A LUMP OF SALT. A CUP OF WATER.` → `WHERE DID IT GO?` → `IT'S IN EVERY SIP.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"Brahman. Not Brahma, the creator god in the stories, and not Brahmin, the priestly class. Different words. Brahman is the name the Upanishads give to the whole: the reality underneath everything, what everything comes from and returns to. Like the salt, it isn't one more thing you can pick out. It's in every taste. The same chapter of the Chandogya has another picture. Rivers flow, some east, some west, and in the end they reach the sea and become the sea. And once there, the Upanishad says, they don't know: I am this river, I am that one. Now the line the father keeps saying to his son, again and again in that chapter: tat tvam asi. That's what you are. Yesterday's word, atman, the self, and today's word, Brahman, the whole: the Upanishad brings them face to face. Hindu thinkers read the line in more than one way. Advaita Vedanta, taught by Shankara, reads it as identity: the self and the whole are not two. Ramanuja's school reads it as one whole with real differences inside it, the self living in God and God as the self's inner ruler. Madhva's school keeps the self and God distinct, the self always dependent on God. These readings sit side by side in the tradition, and nobody here has to choose. Just notice what the picture does. In the Upanishad, the rivers don't dry up somewhere along the way. They become the sea. And today's title borrows the picture for one way of hearing it: a wave that is ocean the whole time, even while it forgets.","screen":["`BRAHMAN — THE WHOLE` · `SALT IN WATER, RIVERS IN THE SEA` · `TAT TVAM ASI — THAT'S WHAT YOU ARE` · `READ MORE THAN ONE WAY` · then, large: `NOT TWO.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min 30","voice":"Here's the practice: let the river reach the sea. Sit and breathe. Bring a river to mind, any one you've seen or can imagine. Let it carry this life, this name, today's worries. Follow it as it moves. Then let it reach the sea, and let the edges go. Not lost: arrived. Rest there, in the big water. Some rest there with one name of God in mind. The bell holds the time.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Brahman. The whole, the reality under everything. Not Brahma, not Brahmin. Eleventh word.","screen":["`BRAHMAN — your 11th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: not two. When you feel cut off from someone today, try the rivers and the sea for one breath. Day eleven.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 11 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: ANYONE WHO TURNS A LIGHT ON.`"]}]},"12":{"day":12,"title":"anyone who turns a light on","word":"guru","hook":"the word got borrowed for salesmen; the old meaning is closer to a lamp","length":"6:00","carry":"thank a teacher today","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY TWELVE.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Yesterday: Brahman. Your line was not two. If it felt too big, that's all right; it's a word to grow into. Today is smaller and closer to home. Day twelve.","screen":["`NOT TWO.` → `DAY 12.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"Marketing guru. Fitness guru. Some guru with an online course and a very confident voice. In everyday English the word has drifted toward a person with something to sell. In Hindu tradition it names something far more personal, and the thing it describes may already be in your life without the word ever being used. A grandparent at the kitchen table. A coach. A teacher who stayed after class. A friend who said one true sentence at exactly the right time. Today's word is for that person.","screen":["`MARKETING GURU. FITNESS GURU.` → `THE OLDER MEANING.` → `ONE TRUE SENTENCE, AT THE RIGHT TIME.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"Guru. A teacher, but in this tradition more than a teacher of facts. A guru is someone who helps you see. An Upanishad called the Advaya Taraka plays with the sound of the word: the syllable gu, it says, means darkness, and ru is what stops it. Wordplay, not a dictionary entry, but a vivid one. A guru is the one who removes the dark. Yesterday you met one without the title. Uddalaka, with the cup of salt water, was a father acting as a guru, teaching his son by letting him taste. In stories like that one, learning happens up close, in a relationship, one person to another. The Mundaka Upanishad says that someone who wants to know should go to a teacher with fuel in hand, firewood for the teacher's fire, a small gift and a sign of willingness to serve. And the teacher, it says, should be learned in the texts and steady in Brahman. Two things there. The student comes humbly. The teacher has to be the real thing. Hindu traditions honor gurus in more than one way. Some follow one teacher for life, inside a long line of teachers. For some, God is the first guru, or the guru within. And on Guru Purnima, a full-moon festival, students thank their teachers. But keep the Advaya Taraka's picture close: someone stood where you were stuck, and the dark lifted. Today's title says it: anyone who turns a light on. A guru isn't only a job title. It can be a moment.","screen":["`GU — DARKNESS, RU — WHAT STOPS IT` · `A FATHER WITH A CUP OF SALT WATER` · `GO WITH FUEL IN HAND` · `A MOMENT, NOT JUST A TITLE` · then, large: `ANYONE WHO TURNS A LIGHT ON.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min 30","voice":"Today, remember one light. Rest your hands in your lap. Bring to mind one time someone helped you see. One sentence, one afternoon, one look. Let their face come. Hear it again, if you can. Then, silently, thank them. If you pray, you can thank God for them too. Sit for a moment in what the room looked like once the light was on. The bell holds the time.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Guru. A teacher who helps you see; in one Upanishad's play on the sound, the one who removes the dark. Twelfth word.","screen":["`GURU — your 12th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: thank a teacher today. The actual person, if you can reach them. Two lines in a message are plenty. Day twelve.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 12 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: WHY 108.`"]}]},"13":{"day":13,"title":"why 108","word":"japa","hook":"beads moving through someone's fingers, and the number on the string","length":"6:00","carry":"count what you want to keep","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY THIRTEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Yesterday: guru. Your line was thank a teacher today. Did a name come to mind? Today a mantra gets a counter. Day thirteen.","screen":["`THANK A TEACHER TODAY.` → `DAY 13.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"Ask why a mala has a hundred and eight beads and you may hear a different answer each time. Watch one in use first. Beads move through someone's fingers, one at a time: wooden beads, or seeds, or stones, on a string, with one larger bead at the top that, by custom, the fingers don't pass over. The lips move without sound. The thumb draws the next bead forward. By tradition, a full Hindu mala carries a hundred and eight beads, plus that one at the top. The number can wait. A better question comes first: what is the person with the beads actually doing?","screen":["`BEADS ON A STRING.` → `LIPS MOVING, NO SOUND.` → `WHY 108?`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"Japa. Repeating a mantra, or a name of God, again and again, often counted. On day three, the Gayatri showed what a mantra is: a tool for the mind. Japa is using that tool again and again. The beads are the counter: one bead, one repetition. The hand keeps count so the mind doesn't have to, and the mind is free to stay with the sound. The Bhagavad Gita names japa among offerings. Here is the verse around it. In the tenth chapter, Krishna names where he can be found. Among things that don't move, the Himalaya. Among words, the one syllable, om. And among offerings, he says, I am the offering of japa. The Sanskrit is yajnanam japa-yajno 'smi. Of all the ways to make an offering, here is one that needs no fire and no flowers. Just a name, said again. Now, why a hundred and eight? People give answers from the sky, from lists of divine names, from the body. None of them comes from a text this lesson can put in front of you, so we'll leave the question open, honestly. But there's something simpler worth noticing about any fixed count. It gives the practice an end. You know when you're done. You don't have to decide, halfway through, whether you've done enough; the string decides. And a practice that can be finished today can be picked up again tomorrow, and the day after. That's how a practice gets kept. So the number on the string may hold mysteries, but it also does a plain job: it turns a good intention into something you can count.","screen":["`JAPA — A NAME, SAID AGAIN` · `THE HAND COUNTS, THE MIND RESTS` · `I AM JAPA, AMONG OFFERINGS` · `A FIXED COUNT GIVES AN END` · then, large: `COUNT WHAT YOU WANT TO KEEP.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min 30","voice":"Here's the practice: count on your fingers. No beads needed. Each finger has three sections. Touch your thumb to the top section of your index finger: one. The middle section: two. The base: three. Then on to the next finger. With each touch, say om silently on the out-breath, or, if you pray, a name of God you use. Go slowly. You won't reach a hundred and eight today, and you don't need to. The bell holds the time.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Japa. Repeating a mantra or a name, counted, so the hand keeps count and the mind can rest. Thirteenth word.","screen":["`JAPA — your 13th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: count what you want to keep. Once today, under a desk or on a train, count a few quiet oms on your fingers. Nobody will know. Day thirteen.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 13 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: WHY IT'S SAID THREE TIMES.`"]}]},"14":{"day":14,"title":"why it's said three times","word":"shanti","hook":"om shanti shanti shanti: three times, never once, and there's a reason","length":"3:00","carry":"peace, peace, peace","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY FOURTEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Yesterday: japa. Your line was count what you want to keep. Two weeks today. Today, a word you've probably heard said three times in a row.","screen":["`COUNT WHAT YOU WANT TO KEEP.` → `TWO WEEKS.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Om shanti shanti shanti. It closes a lot of Hindu prayers, and more than a few yoga classes. Not once, not twice: three times. That isn't just for the rhythm. Traditional teachers read the three as a list. First, a guess at the word.","screen":["`OM SHANTI SHANTI SHANTI.` → `WHY THREE?`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"55 sec","voice":"Shanti means peace, the settled kind: calm water with nothing stirring it. The Bhagavad Gita puts it bluntly: for someone without peace, how can there be happiness? Now the three. Traditional teachers read the three shantis as three kinds of trouble, the three places a bad day can come from. Trouble from forces beyond anyone's control: storms, floods, the world. Trouble from other beings: the friend who didn't call, the driver who cut in. And trouble from within: your own body and your own mind. Three sources, three shantis. The teachers add something practical. There's little anyone can do about the first, more about the second, and the third is the one most within reach, and the easiest to skip. So the chant isn't padding. It goes down the list: peace out there, peace between us, peace in here.","screen":["`SHANTI — THE SETTLED KIND.` · `THE WORLD.` · `OTHER PEOPLE.` · `YOUR OWN MIND.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Three breaths with the bell, one shanti on each, said silently. First out-breath: toward the world. Second: toward one person you're not at peace with. You don't have to forgive them. Third: toward your own mind.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Shanti. Peace, the settled kind, said three times: the world, other people, your own mind. Fourteenth word. Puja, dharma, atman, Brahman, guru, japa, shanti: that's week two.","screen":["`SHANTI — your 14th word` → `WEEK TWO. DONE.`"]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"10 sec","voice":"Your line: peace, peace, peace. Next time something rattles you, there are three places it could have come from. Next week brings the doing words. Day fourteen.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 14 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: HANUMAN'S WHOLE PERSONALITY.`"]}]},"15":{"day":15,"title":"Hanuman's whole personality","word":"seva","hook":"a mountain carried across the sky, because one herb was needed","length":"6:30","carry":"do something for someone, quietly","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY FIFTEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Yesterday, shanti. Your line was peace, peace, peace. Which of the three rattled you most? Week three starts now, and it opens with words you do with your hands. Day fifteen.","screen":["`PEACE, PEACE, PEACE.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"On a battlefield in Lanka, Lakshmana, Rama's brother, lies wounded, barely breathing. A healer says there is a cure: certain herbs that grow on a mountain far to the north, and they have to be here fast. Hanuman goes. He crosses the sky, finds the mountain, and then he stops. The herbs are hiding from him, the story says, and he can't tell which ones are needed. There's no time to go back and ask. So he solves it another way. He lifts the whole peak out of the ground and carries it back through the air, herbs and all. Lakshmana breathes. Hanuman has a monkey's face, the strength of the wind, and a picture of him hangs in homes and shops. Today's word is why.","screen":["`A BROTHER WOUNDED. NO TIME TO LOSE.` → `HE CAN'T TELL WHICH HERB.` → `SO HE BRINGS THE MOUNTAIN.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"Seva. Service. Not service as a job title, and not help with a receipt attached. Seva is doing what's needed because it's needed, with the self stepped out of the way. Hanuman is the tradition's living picture of it. Notice what the mountain story leaves out. There's no speech. No moment where he explains how hard it was. The whole story points at one thing: a man on the ground starts breathing again. The strength is enormous, and it's all aimed outward. The Ramayana does this with him more than once. When he leaps the ocean and finds Sita in Lanka, what he finally puts in Rama's hand is hers, not his: her jewel, and her message. Later devotional stories push the picture even further; people tell of him opening his own chest to show that Rama and Sita live inside it. Whatever he has, it's for someone else. And seva doesn't need a mountain. In temples and homes it's very ordinary work. Washing the floor before the festival. Chopping vegetables for a meal that strangers will eat. Sitting with a sick neighbor so the family can sleep. Nobody hands out credit for it, and that's part of the point. There's something else folded into the word. Some who practice it say seva done quietly works on the one doing it too: for as long as the hands are busy with someone else's need, the head gets a rest from itself. Hanuman's gifts were huge. The practice works with whatever size yours are.","screen":["`SEVA — SERVICE, SELF OUT OF THE WAY.` · `NO SPEECH. A MAN BREATHES AGAIN.` · `HER JEWEL, NOT HIS ADVENTURE.` · `A FLOOR, A MEAL, A NIGHT'S SLEEP.` · then, large: `WHATEVER HE HAS, IT'S FOR SOMEONE ELSE.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min 30","voice":"Today's practice: find your herb. Let your shoulders drop. Bring one thing to mind that you're good at. Cooking. Listening. Fixing a bike. Staying calm in a mess. Now let one person come to mind who could use exactly that this week. Don't plan it all out. Just decide: I'll do it, and I won't mention it. Then rest in the decision for the length of the sit. If you pray, you can offer the work before you do it. The bell holds the time.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Seva. Service done because it's needed, with the self out of the way. Hanuman carrying the mountain. Fifteenth word.","screen":["`SEVA — your 15th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: do something for someone, quietly. One small thing today. No post. No mention. Just a mountain moved, and nobody told. Day fifteen.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 15 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: LOVE AS A METHOD.`"]}]},"16":{"day":16,"title":"love as a method","word":"bhakti","hook":"a leaf, a flower, a fruit, a little water: what the Gita says is enough","length":"6:30","carry":"devotion is a verb","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY SIXTEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Yesterday, seva, and Hanuman with a mountain in his hands. Your line was do something for someone, quietly. If you did it, did some part of you want to tell somebody? Day sixteen.","screen":["`DO SOMETHING FOR SOMEONE, QUIETLY.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"A leaf. A flower. A piece of fruit. A little water. That list came up on day eight, at the home shrine, and back then the point was how small the gifts are. Today, read the rest of the sentence. In the ninth chapter of the Bhagavad Gita, Krishna says that whoever offers him one of these with love, he accepts it, because it was offered with love. The gift isn't what makes it count. The love is. And the Sanskrit word for that love sits right inside the verse. A whole way of walking this path is built on it, a way full of songs, drums, bells and late nights of singing. It's today's word.","screen":["`A LEAF, A FLOWER, AGAIN.` → `OFFERED WITH LOVE.` → `THE WORD INSIDE THE VERSE.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"Bhakti. Devotion. Love turned toward God and practiced on purpose, the way you'd practice an instrument. You've spent some of this camp with the head: atman, Brahman, the self and the whole. Bhakti is the heart, and the Gita treats it as a full path of its own. In the Gita's telling, nobody has to master the ideas first. The way in is love. Devotees aim that love at God in different forms, Krishna, Rama, Shiva, the Goddess, and the move is the same. Tradition remembers a woman who showed it. Mirabai was a princess in what is now Rajasthan, and the stories say that from childhood she thought of Krishna as her own. Married into a royal family, she kept singing to him in the temple, in public, with ordinary devotees. Her in-laws, the stories go, were furious. They sent her poison, and it did her no harm. Whatever you make of those details, her songs are still sung. Here's why bhakti is a method and not only a mood. Moods come and go. The lamp gets lit anyway. The song gets sung on the tired night too. Some who practice it say the feeling catches up with the doing, not the other way around. Go back to the leaf: what the verse looks for is the love it's offered with, and love, on this path, is something you keep doing. That's part of why this path can be so loud. Bells, drums, a crowd singing the same name over and over. That's bhakti with the volume up.","screen":["`BHAKTI — LOVE, PRACTICED ON PURPOSE.` · `THE HEART IS A PATH TOO.` · `MIRABAI KEPT SINGING.` · `THE LAMP GETS LIT ANYWAY.` · then, large: `DEVOTION IS A VERB.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min 30","voice":"The practice today: offer a leaf. Find one small thing within reach. A sip of water, a flower, a breath, even just your open hand. Hold it for a moment. If you pray, you can offer it, with love, to God in whatever form you know. If you don't, offer it to whatever you love most in the world, and let the feeling be as big as it wants to be. Nobody's watching. The bell holds the time.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Bhakti. Devotion. Love you practice, not only love you feel. A leaf, offered whole. Sixteenth word.","screen":["`BHAKTI — your 16th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: devotion is a verb. Today, do one loving thing on a day you don't feel like it. That's the method, all of it. Day sixteen.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 16 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE COURAGE NOT TO STRIKE.`"]}]},"17":{"day":17,"title":"the courage not to strike","word":"ahimsa","hook":"the yoga list that starts before any pose: not-harming","length":"6:30","carry":"do no harm — start with your mouth","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY SEVENTEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Yesterday, bhakti, love practiced like an instrument. Your line was devotion is a verb. Did you do the loving thing on a day you didn't feel like it? Today's word asks for a different kind of strength. Day seventeen.","screen":["`DEVOTION IS A VERB.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"A message comes in late at night. It's unfair, and it stings, and your thumbs already know the reply. The sharp one. The one that would land. Here's a strange fact about yoga. Before the Yoga Sutras of Patanjali teach anything about posture, they set out a short list of restraints, the ground the rest is built on, and the first word on that list isn't stretch, or breathe. It's not-harming. So the first restraint in that text has something to say to those thumbs.","screen":["`A MESSAGE. A SHARP REPLY READY.` → `YOGA BEFORE THE POSES.` → `THE FIRST WORD: NOT-HARMING.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"Ahimsa. Himsa is harm, violence. Ahimsa is its opposite: not-harming. And it's easy to hear that as a soft word, a word for people who never stand up for anything. Gandhi, who put this word beside truth at the center of his public life, heard it the other way around. He was not a quiet man. He confronted an empire, and he refused the one weapon everyone expected him to pick up. Ahimsa takes nerve. When something hits a person, the body often wants to hit back, and ahimsa is the half-second where you don't add more harm to a moment that already has some. The Jains, close neighbors on the same soil, hold ahimsa at the heart of their path too. Now look at what Patanjali says comes of it. The Yoga Sutras teach that when someone is truly settled in not-harming, hostility falls away in their presence. Not because they won the argument. Because they stopped feeding it. Some teachers name three places ahimsa lives: in thought, in word, and in deed. Deeds are the obvious ones. Today's line starts with the middle one, words, because that's where the half-second is easiest to miss. The cutting joke. The thing said about someone who isn't in the room. The reply sent at night. One more layer. The vow points inward too. A voice in the head that calls a person worthless is himsa as well. Not-harming includes the one doing the not-harming.","screen":["`AHIMSA — NOT-HARMING.` · `NOT SOFT. IT TAKES NERVE.` · `HOSTILITY FALLS AWAY.` · `THOUGHT, WORD, DEED.` · then, large: `START WITH YOUR MOUTH.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min 30","voice":"Now, find the half-second. Bring back the last time you said something sharp, to someone or about someone. Don't punish yourself for it; that would be harm too. Just replay it, slowly, and this time stop at the moment before the words came out. Feel the push to strike. Let it pass through without acting on it. What could have been said instead? Maybe nothing. Rest there. The bell holds the time.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Ahimsa. Not-harming, in thought, word and deed. The courage not to strike. Seventeenth word.","screen":["`AHIMSA — your 17th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: do no harm — start with your mouth. Today, let one sharp sentence go unsaid, and notice what happens to the room. Day seventeen.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 17 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: WHY THE WORLD LOOKS SOLID.`"]}]},"18":{"day":18,"title":"why the world looks solid","word":"maya","hook":"a rope on a dark path, and the snake that was never there","length":"6:30","carry":"hold it lightly","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY EIGHTEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Yesterday, ahimsa, the courage not to strike. Your line was do no harm — start with your mouth. Did a sharp sentence go unsaid? Today we go back to the big ideas for a day. Day eighteen.","screen":["`DO NO HARM — START WITH YOUR MOUTH.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"Dusk, on a path, and something is coiled across it. Your heart jumps before your mind has a word for it. Snake. You freeze. Your pulse is real. The fear is real. Then someone brings a lamp, and it's a rope. Just a rope, lying where it always was. Nothing about the rope changed. The snake was never there. And yet for a few seconds it ran your whole body. Teachers in this tradition tell that small story because it holds a very big word. A thing that is not what it seems, and works on you anyway.","screen":["`SOMETHING COILED ON THE PATH.` → `THE FEAR IS REAL.` → `A LAMP. A ROPE.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"Maya. It often gets translated as illusion, and that one word causes a lot of confusion. It sounds like the tradition is saying the world is fake. Your kitchen, your friends, your job, none of it real. That isn't what it says. The Svetasvatara Upanishad puts it this way: know that nature is maya, and that the great Lord is the one who works the maya. So maya isn't nothing. It's the power that makes the world appear the way it does, so convincingly that we stop looking past it. Schools of the tradition read this differently, and they've argued about it with great care. Advaita Vedanta, the school of Shankara, says the world we see is an appearance laid over Brahman, the way the snake is laid over the rope. The rope is what's really there. Other schools, like those of Ramanuja and Madhva, hold that the world is fully real, made by God, and that maya names God's wondrous power. Both readings agree on one thing. The surface is not the whole story. And that's where the word walks into an ordinary day. A post makes someone's life look perfect. A comment feels like an attack on all of you. A bad morning looks like a bad life. Each one is a snake on the path, until the lamp comes. Neither reading asks anyone to stop living in the world. Only to stop gripping it as if the surface were everything. Hold it lightly.","screen":["`NOT FAKE. NOT THE WHOLE STORY.` · `THE POWER THAT MAKES IT APPEAR.` · `SNAKE ON A ROPE, OR GOD'S POWER?` · `A POST. A COMMENT. A BAD MORNING.` · then, large: `HOLD IT LIGHTLY.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min 30","voice":"Today's practice: bring the lamp. Breathe once, slowly. Recall one thing this week that made your heart jump. A message, a look, a number, a picture of someone else's life. Feel the jump again, just a little. Don't push it away. Now look at it again, slowly, as if someone had brought a lamp. What is actually there? Let the tightness loosen as much as it wants to. The bell holds the time.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Maya. The power that makes the world appear, so well that we stop looking past the surface. The snake that turns out to be rope. Eighteenth word.","screen":["`MAYA — your 18th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: hold it lightly. Today, when something makes you jump, take one breath and ask: snake, or rope? Day eighteen.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 18 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE EXIT DOOR.`"]}]},"19":{"day":19,"title":"the exit door","word":"moksha","hook":"a boy at Death's door turns down every treasure for one question","length":"6:30","carry":"freedom is the point","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY NINETEEN.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Yesterday, maya, and the snake that was a rope. Your line was hold it lightly. Did you catch a snake on the path? Today, the word for where this whole path is headed. Day nineteen.","screen":["`HOLD IT LIGHTLY.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"A boy named Nachiketa is sitting at the door of Death's house. In the Katha Upanishad, his father, in a burst of temper, has said: I give you to Death. So the boy goes. Yama, the lord of death, is away, and Nachiketa waits on the doorstep without food for three nights. When Yama comes home, he's embarrassed to have kept a guest waiting, and he offers the boy three wishes. The first two are easy. The third is a question: what happens to a person after death? Yama tries to talk him out of it. Ask for something else, he says. Ask for long life, sons and grandsons, cattle, gold, a kingdom. Nachiketa says no.","screen":["`A BOY AT DEATH'S DOOR.` → `THREE WISHES.` → `TAKE THE GOLD INSTEAD.` → `NO.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"Nachiketa's answer is the heart of today's word. He tells Death that all those treasures wear out, that wealth will never satisfy a person, that even a long life is short. Keep your horses, he says. I want to know. And Yama, impressed, begins to teach him. That turn, from the list of things to the thing under the list, points straight at today's word. Moksha means release. Liberation. Freedom. The tradition speaks of the soul moving through birth after birth, bound by its own actions and desires, and moksha is the knot finally coming untied. Hindu thought names several aims of a good life: dharma, doing what's yours to do; artha, making a living; kama, love and pleasure. None of those are bad. Moksha sits beyond them, as the freedom the others point toward. What that freedom is, the schools describe differently. Advaita Vedanta says it's waking up to what day ten and day eleven already hinted: that atman, the self, is Brahman, and always was. Devotional schools, such as those centered on Krishna, describe it as living forever in the loving presence of God, never dissolved, never alone. Some schools teach that a person can be free before death, liberated while still living. Notice the shape of Nachiketa's choice. He doesn't hate gold. He just won't trade the real question for it. That's the word in an ordinary day too: the moment you see that the next thing on the list was never going to be enough.","screen":["`KEEP YOUR HORSES. I WANT TO KNOW.` · `MOKSHA — RELEASE. THE KNOT UNTIED.` · `BEYOND DUTY, WEALTH AND PLEASURE.` · `ATMAN IS BRAHMAN, OR LOVE WITH GOD.` · then, large: `FREEDOM IS THE POINT.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min 30","voice":"Here's the practice: set one thing down. Sit and breathe. Notice one thing you want right now. A message back, a better week, a number on a screen. Let the wanting be there. Now, just for the length of this sit, set it down beside you. Not forever. Just: for now, I don't need it. Breathe, and notice what's left when the wanting rests. If you pray, you can hold that space open to God. The bell holds the time.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Moksha. Release. Liberation. The knot untied, the freedom the other aims of life point toward. Nineteenth word.","screen":["`MOKSHA — your 19th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: freedom is the point. Once today, when you catch yourself chasing the next thing, set it down for one breath, the way Nachiketa set down the gold. Day nineteen.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 19 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: GOOD COMPANY.`"]}]},"20":{"day":20,"title":"good company","word":"satsang","hook":"an old chain of verses that starts with who you sit with and ends in freedom","length":"6:30","carry":"good company changes you","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY TWENTY.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Yesterday, moksha, and a boy who told Death to keep his gold. Your line was freedom is the point. Did you set something down, even for a breath? One more word before the camp ends. Day twenty.","screen":["`FREEDOM IS THE POINT.`"]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"Yesterday's word was the top of the mountain: freedom. Fair question, then. Where do you start? There's an old Sanskrit song called Bhaja Govindam, traditionally credited to the teacher Shankara, and one of its verses answers that question like a row of dominoes. Each step knocks over the next, all the way up to freedom. You'd expect the first domino to be something hard. Years of study. Fasting. A cave in the mountains. It isn't. The very first domino is who you spend your time with.","screen":["`WHERE DO YOU START?` → `A ROW OF DOMINOES.` → `THE FIRST ONE: YOUR COMPANY.`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"Satsang. Sat means true, real, good. Sang means company, being together. Satsang is good company, the company of what's true and of people turned toward it. Here's the chain from Bhaja Govindam, in plain words. From good company comes letting go. From letting go comes freedom from delusion. From freedom from delusion comes a mind that stays steady. And from a steady mind comes freedom while you're still alive. Look at what's in that chain. Delusion: think of the snake on the rope from day eighteen. Freedom is moksha from yesterday. And the thing that starts it all isn't a technique. It's a room. In Hindu life, a satsang is often an actual gathering. People meet in a home or a temple hall to sing, to hear a teacher, to read a text together, to sit in silence side by side. Nobody has to be advanced. You come, you sit, you listen. The idea underneath the verse is simple and a little uncomfortable: company shapes a person. A friend's worries can become your worries; a grandmother's patience can become your patience. The verse doesn't say go find perfect people. It puts company first in the chain, and leaves the question with you: what is your company doing to you, and how much time do you spend near what's true? That first domino has a name and a face.","screen":["`SAT — TRUE. SANG — COMPANY.` · `GOOD COMPANY, THEN LETTING GO, THEN FREEDOM.` · `A ROOM, NOT A TECHNIQUE.` · `WE TAKE THE SHAPE OF OUR COMPANY.` · then, large: `GOOD COMPANY CHANGES YOU.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min 30","voice":"Here's the practice: sit in company. Sit and breathe. Bring to mind one person whose presence makes you steadier. A friend, a grandparent, a teacher, someone in a story. Let their face come. Notice what happens to your breathing near them. Then widen it, to anyone, anywhere, who stops today to pray or to sit still. Let yourself be one of them. If you pray, you can hold them all before God. The bell holds the time.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"Satsang. Good company. The company of what's true, and the first domino on the way to freedom. Twentieth word.","screen":["`SATSANG — your 20th word`."]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: good company changes you. Today, spend ten unhurried minutes with one person who steadies you. In person, if you can. Day twenty.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 20 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE MOUNTAIN AHEAD.`"]}]},"21":{"day":21,"title":"the mountain ahead","word":"the Gita","hook":"back to the battlefield: a warrior drops his bow, and the conversation begins","length":"6:45","carry":"you have the right to act. Not to the fruit","segments":[{"type":"the bell","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY TWENTY-ONE.`"]},{"type":"review","duration":"20 sec","voice":"Yesterday, satsang. Your line was good company changes you. Now, the whole camp, quickly: Ganesha, om, Gayatri, namaste, arti, ishta devata, karma, puja, dharma, atman, Brahman, guru, japa, shanti, seva, bhakti, ahimsa, maya, moksha, satsang. Twenty words. Today makes twenty-one. Day twenty-one.","screen":["`GOOD COMPANY CHANGES YOU.` → the strand, twenty words lit."]},{"type":"the hook","duration":"50 sec","voice":"Go back to day nine. A battlefield, two armies drawn up, conch shells blowing. Arjuna, a great archer, asks his charioteer to drive him out between the lines so he can see who he's about to fight. He looks across and sees teachers, uncles, cousins, friends. His mouth goes dry. His bow slips from his hand. And he sits down in the chariot and says he will not fight. We left him there, with his driver, Krishna, beside him. Today we find out what happens next. Because the whole conversation that follows has a name, and it's the last word of this camp.","screen":["`BACK TO THE BATTLEFIELD.` → `HIS BOW SLIPS.` → `WHAT DOES THE DRIVER SAY?`"]},{"type":"the teach","duration":"2 min 15","voice":"The Bhagavad Gita. The Song of the Lord. It sits inside the great epic, the Mahabharata, and it's a conversation between two people in a chariot. On day nine you heard that the driver is Krishna, and a great deal more than a driver. Krishna does more than give orders. He teaches. He talks about the self that cannot be killed, which you met as atman. He talks about acting without grabbing, about loving God, about seeing clearly. Look back at this week and you'll hear echoes. Action with the self out of the way, the way seva works. Love as a path of its own, bhakti. The Gita holds these paths side by side. Then the line this camp saved for last. In the second chapter Krishna says, in plain words: your right is to the action alone, never to its fruits. Don't let the fruit be your reason for acting. And the verse keeps going: don't let that become a reason to do nothing, either. It isn't a way out of the fight. It's a way to stand up and act without being owned by the result. And near the end of the Gita comes a surprise. After everything, Krishna tells Arjuna to think it over fully, and then do as he chooses. He hands the choice back. In the fourth camp you'll read this text itself, line by line. For now you get the door, and the sentence that opens it.","screen":["`THE SONG OF THE LORD, IN A CHARIOT.` · `THE WHOLE CAMP IS IN THERE.` · `NOT A WAY OUT. A WAY TO STAND UP.` · `THINK IT OVER. THEN CHOOSE.` · then, large: `YOU HAVE THE RIGHT TO ACT. NOT TO THE FRUIT.`"]},{"type":"the practice","duration":"2 min 30","voice":"Here's the practice: pick up the bow. Sit and breathe. Press your palms together at your chest, the way you did on day one. Bring to mind one thing that's yours to do this week, the thing you've been sitting down in the chariot about. Picture doing it, fully. Then open your hand, and let go of how it turns out. The action is yours. The fruit isn't. If you pray, you can offer the result to God. The bell holds the time.","screen":[]},{"type":"the word","duration":"25 sec","voice":"The Gita. The Song of the Lord, a conversation in a chariot that gets a warrior back on his feet. Twenty-first word, and the door to the mountain.","screen":["`THE GITA — your 21st word` → the strand complete → `CAMP 1. FIRST WORDS. DONE.`"]},{"type":"the carry","duration":"15 sec","voice":"Your line: you have the right to act. Not to the fruit. Do one thing today that's yours to do, and let the result go. Tomorrow the stories begin, with a goddess, a bath, and a boy made of earth. Day twenty-one.","screen":[]},{"type":"the close","duration":null,"voice":"","screen":["`DAY 21 COUNTS.` → `TOMORROW: THE BOY AT THE DOOR.`"]}]}}};
